course: dictionary
lesson: 8736
title: Serious: one word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the sentence around it shows you which meaning to use. «This time it is serious: he has been missing for some days now.» A missing person is a big problem, so serious means bad enough to worry about.
explain: The same word can mean something different in another sentence. «She gave me a serious look and did not smile.» Here serious describes a face: the person is thoughtful and not joking. The clue is the missing smile.
explain: Serious can also mean important and needing careful thought, as in a serious talk. Always check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have chosen well.
fact: Serious means bad or dangerous enough to worry about, like «a serious injury».
fact: A serious person is thoughtful and not joking or laughing.
fact: If you are serious about something, you really mean it and are not pretending.
fact: «Seriously» is the adverb: «He was seriously hurt» means he was badly hurt.
fact: In safety talk, «serious» marks danger that needs an adult or emergency help, such as «a serious allergy».
exercise: «A bump on the head swells, but the size of the bump does not show how serious the injury is.» What does serious mean here? | How many colours the bump has | How loudly the child cried | How big the bump is | *How bad or dangerous the injury is
exercise: Mia asks, «Are you serious?» What does she want to know? | Are you feeling sad? | *Do you really mean it? | Is it raining yet? | Do you want to play outside?
exercise: «He spoke in a serious voice.» What does serious suggest about him? | *He is thoughtful and not joking | He is fast asleep | He is laughing at a funny story | He is hungry for lunch
test: «Nadia said she was serious about joining the team.» What does serious mean here? | She is very sad about the team | She is too tired to play | She is joking with her friends | *She really means it and is not pretending
test: «The doctor said the burn was serious, so we went to hospital.» Why did they go to hospital? | The burn was a tiny mark | *The burn was bad enough to need help | The doctor wanted a snack | The burn had a nice colour
exam: «He is seriously hurt after the fall.» What does seriously mean here? | Slowly and quietly | Happily and with a big smile | Just for fun | *Badly, so he needs help
